      "B": "The forward hedge is always cheaper because it locks in a known rate. Money market hedges involve borrowing and lending spreads that make them inherently more expensive.",
      "C": "Bridgeway should remain unhedged because the euro has historically strengthened against the dollar. Over the long term, hedging costs reduce returns without providing meaningful risk reduction.",
      "D": "The money market hedge is always cheaper because it captures the interest rate differential. Under covered interest arbitrage, the forward rate should reflect the interest rate differential, making money market hedges redundant."
