    "ExplanationCorrect": "International finance distinguishes price risk (FX rates) from sovereign risk (expropriation, transfer and convertibility restrictions, breach of contract). Forward contracts and FX hedges address the former, not the latter. Political risk insurance (e.g., OPIC/MIGA-style coverage) compensates for expropriation, political violence, and transfer inconvertibility for insured perils. A joint venture with a reputable local partner raises the political cost of expropriation and may satisfy local-ownership requirements. Local-currency debt matches the currency of operating cash flows, creating a natural hedge that reduces the net asset exposure subject to devaluation and lowers the amount of cash that must be repatriated through capital controls. Business interpretation: Cascade should layer the three — insurance for tail sovereign events, JV structure for alignment and legitimacy, and balance-sheet matching for ongoing currency and transfer mitigation — rather than relying on any single financial hedge. Common trap: treating a forward contract as a political-risk hedge confuses fixing an exchange rate with protecting property rights.",
