    "ExplanationWrongC": "Host-government jurisdiction extends to all assets physically located within its borders regardless of currency denomination. Capital controls and expropriation orders can apply to dollar cash held in local banks, and local courts enforce the controls. Dollar denomination does not create extraterritorial immunity, and holding excess idle dollar cash also incurs opportunity cost and may itself attract scrutiny as a target for transfer restrictions.",
    "ExplanationWrongD": "Moving cash offshore does not eliminate transfer risk at zero cost. The subsidiary needs working cash locally to pay employees, suppliers, and taxes; offshore funds cannot fund daily operations without re-entering through the controlled channel. Moreover, aggressive offshore cash pooling to evade controls may violate local regulations and damage the joint-venture relationship. Capital controls are designed to restrict exactly this circumvention, and host authorities can penalize firms that systematically bypass them.",
